<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><!--
     AresLauncher: Niagara-style app-list row (icon-left, label-dominant, generous row height).

     Deliberately a separate file from all_apps_icon.xml, which is shared with the Taskbar's
     all-apps sheet via BaseAllAppsAdapter; editing that in place restyles the Taskbar too.
     See design/niagara-app-list.md §3 and design/implementation-scope.md §8.

     Two attributes from the stock layout are deliberately NOT set here:
       - launcher:centerVertically is incompatible with layoutHorizontal. Its onMeasure() path sums
         icon + padding + text height, which only holds when the icon is stacked ABOVE the text; in
         horizontal mode that over-estimates content height and yields a large bogus paddingTop.
       - the style's android:gravity="center_horizontal" (inherited from BaseIconUnBounded) is
         overridden below, otherwise the label floats centred in the leftover width instead of
         sitting next to the icon.
     Both diagnosed in design/gesture-transition-reassessment.md §4.

     textColor is set to workspaceTextColor rather than inheriting the style's textColorSecondary.
     BaseIconUnBounded assumes all-apps sits on an opaque sheet, so its default is a dark-on-light
     colour. Since the CONVERGENCE TARGET restyle the pane draws directly on the wallpaper with no
     sheet, that default rendered dark-on-dark and was barely legible. workspaceTextColor is the
     attribute the home list already resolves against the wallpaper, so both surfaces now agree.

     ROW HEIGHT IS A FLOOR, NOT A FIXED HEIGHT (2026-09-02). The label is sized in `sp`, so it grows
     with the user's font scale; Android 14+ non-linear font scaling reaches 2.0x, and Display Size
     (largest) multiplies density by a further ~1.3x. Both are ordinary Settings toggles that need no
     special hardware. At 2.0x a 17sp label needs ~52.2dp of line box plus padding, inside what used
     to be a hard 52dp box that also has to hold a 32dp icon - so the label clipped, silently, for
     anyone who had turned font size up.

     `minHeight` keeps the row at exactly 52dp at the default scale, so the measured row is unchanged
     for anyone who has not: no visible difference on the owner's device. It only grows when the text
     genuinely needs the room. Asserted by :ares-geom-tests, which parses THIS file and checks the
     height is wrap_content with a minHeight rather than a fixed dimen.

     Watch the `layoutHorizontal` measure path if this misbehaves: BubbleTextView is documented to
     over-estimate content height in that mode and yield a large bogus paddingTop, and wrap_content
     sends the row straight through it.
-->
<com.android.launcher3.BubbleTextView xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:launcher="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
    style="@style/BaseIcon.AllApps"
    android:id="@+id/icon"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
    android:minHeight="@dimen/ares_app_row_height"
    android:drawablePadding="@dimen/ares_app_row_drawable_padding"
    android:ellipsize="end"
    android:gravity="start|center_vertical"
    android:maxLines="1"
    android:paddingStart="@dimen/ares_app_row_padding_horizontal"
    android:paddingEnd="@dimen/ares_app_row_padding_horizontal"
    android:textColor="?attr/workspaceTextColor"
    android:textSize="@dimen/ares_app_row_text_size"
    launcher:iconDisplay="all_apps"
    launcher:iconSizeOverride="@dimen/ares_app_row_icon_size"
    launcher:layoutHorizontal="true" />
